message.content:
Create ONE new file at `/opt/nxeon/lib/ai-build.ts` — a PURE TypeScript data module (no imports, no "use server"/"use client", no functions that do IO; only typed exported consts + the type definitions). It powers a customer-portal "Build with Claude Code" education hub for a VPS hosting company called **Nxeon**. Customers get a VPS with Claude Code (Anthropic's CLI) pre-installed; this content teaches them to build websites AND apps by copy-pasting fill-in-the-blank prompts.

The file MUST start with EXACTLY these type definitions (copy verbatim), then the data:

```ts
// Pure content module for the "Build with Claude Code" hub. No IO, no imports.

export type TerminalLine = {
  kind: "cmd" | "out" | "claude" | "comment" | "input" | "ok";
  text: string;
};

export type SetupStep = {
  title: string;
  body: string;
  terminal?: TerminalLine[];
  note?: string;
};

export type SetupFlow = {
  id: string;           // "subscription" | "apikey"
  label: string;        // e.g. "Use your Claude subscription"
  tagline: string;
  best: string;         // one line: who this is best for
  steps: SetupStep[];
};

export type Category = {
  id: string;           // slug, matches PromptTemplate.category
  label: string;
  emoji: string;
  blurb: string;
};

export type Placeholder = {
  key: string;          // token WITHOUT braces, e.g. "BUSINESS_NAME"
  label: string;        // human label
  example: string;      // realistic default example
};

export type PromptTemplate = {
  id: string;           // slug
  title: string;
  blurb: string;        // one sentence on what it builds
  emoji: string;
  category: string;     // matches Category.id
  tags: string[];
  stack: string;        // e.g. "Next.js + Tailwind"
  prompt: string;       // the full prompt, with {PLACEHOLDER} tokens using curly braces
  placeholders: Placeholder[];
  followups: string[];  // 2-3 suggested next prompts (plain strings)
};

export type Guide = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  blurb: string;
  emoji: string;
  steps: { title: string; body: string; code?: string }[];
};

export type QuickStep = { n: number; title: string; body: string };
```

Then export these consts (exact names):

1. `export const QUICKSTART: QuickStep[]` — 3 steps: (1) SSH into your server (or open the web console), (2) run `claude`, (3) describe what you want to build in plain English. Short and punchy.

2. `export const SETUP_FLOWS: SetupFlow[]` — EXACTLY two flows:
   - id "subscription", label "Use your Claude subscription", best "Best if you already pay for Claude Pro or Max — no extra cost per project." Steps: connect to the server (show `ssh root@your-server-ip` terminal), start Claude (`claude`), choose "Subscription" at the login prompt, open the printed URL in a browser + log in to claude.ai and paste the code back, then verify with a first prompt. Use realistic `terminal` arrays (traffic-light-less; the UI draws the window). Show the OAuth-style login flow Claude Code actually uses (it prints a URL and asks you to authorize, then paste a code). Include a `note` warning: your subscription usage limits apply; this uses YOUR personal Claude account.
   - id "apikey", label "Use an Anthropic API key", best "Best for pay-as-you-go usage or if you don't have a Claude subscription." Steps: get a key from console.anthropic.com (Settings → API Keys), connect to server, set it with `export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...` (and how to persist it in `~/.bashrc`), run `claude`, verify. Include a `note` that API usage is billed per token by Anthropic, not by Nxeon.

   Make the terminal lines realistic and educational. Use kind "cmd" for commands the user types, "out" for plain output, "claude" for Claude's responses, "comment" for `# explanatory comments`, "ok" for success lines, "input" for prompts asking for input.

3. `export const CATEGORIES: Category[]` — exactly these ids in this order: "websites", "webapps", "apis", "automation", "content", "games". Give each a good emoji + one-sentence blurb.

4. `export const PROMPTS: PromptTemplate[]` — AT LEAST 26 templates, genuinely useful and detailed, spread across all 6 categories (at least 3 per category). The VERY FIRST entry must be the flagship: a **beautiful spa/wellness website** (id "spa-website", category "websites") with a rich, opinionated prompt (modern responsive design, hero with booking CTA, services grid with prices, about, testimonials, gallery, contact form, mobile-friendly, tasteful color palette, smooth scroll). Placeholders like {BUSINESS_NAME}, {CITY}, {SERVICES}, {BRAND_COLOR}. 
   Other websites: restaurant site, photographer/portfolio, real-estate listings, SaaS landing page, personal blog, local-business (plumber/dentist) site, event/wedding page.
   webapps: to-do/task manager, booking/appointment app, CRM, invoicing app, habit tracker, url shortener, admin dashboard, notes app.
   apis: REST API with auth, webhook receiver, image-resize API, a Stripe-payment backend, a Discord bot backend.
   automation: web scraper, scheduled report emailer, Discord bot, Telegram bot, file-backup script, social-media auto-poster.
   content: AI blog-writer script, resume/CV site, newsletter template, landing-page A/B copy, SEO audit script.
   games: browser game (snake/2048-style), multiplayer lobby, quiz game, Minecraft plugin.
   Choose sensible ones to reach ≥26 total, ≥3 per category. Each prompt should be 3-6 sentences, specify a stack (Next.js + Tailwind for web, Node/Express or Python for backends, etc.), mention responsive/accessible/production-ready where relevant, and END with an instruction like "Then run it and tell me the URL to preview it." Each needs 2-4 placeholders with realistic examples and 2-3 followups (e.g. "Add a dark mode toggle", "Connect a contact form to my email", "Deploy it so it's live on my domain").

5. `export const GUIDES: Guide[]` — 6 guides, each 3-5 steps. Topics: "Put your site online with your domain" (point DNS, run on port 80/443, nginx or the built-in), "Keep your app running 24/7" (pm2 or systemd), "Add a database" (Postgres/SQLite via Claude), "Save your work to GitHub", "Let Claude find and fix a bug" (describe the error, let it debug), "Give Claude a screenshot or design to copy". Steps may include short `code` snippets. Keep practical and beginner-friendly.

Constraints:
- Real, high-quality copy — this is customer-facing. No lorem, no TODO placeholders.
- Use ASCII/simple punctuation; keep emojis to single glyphs.
- Prompt tokens use curly braces exactly matching the `placeholders[].key` (e.g. prompt contains `{BUSINESS_NAME}` and placeholders has key "BUSINESS_NAME").
- The file MUST typecheck: run `cd /opt/nxeon && npx tsc --noEmit 2>&1 | grep ai-build` and ensure ZERO errors for ai-build.ts before you finish. Fix any that appear.
- Do NOT modify any other file. Do NOT run git. Only create /opt/nxeon/lib/ai-build.ts.

When done, report the total count of PROMPTS and confirm tsc is clean for the file.

